WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.660
Diet shifts lowered biological age markers in older adults after four weeks. This is Planetarian Daily,

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:13.760
episode 185. Older adults who altered the balance of fat, carbohydrates, and animal protein,

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:20.180
showed reduced biological age after four weeks. Participants on an omnivorous, lower fat,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:26.660
higher carbohydrate diet recorded the strongest effect. The study tracked changes in established biological

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:32.420
biological age indicators following controlled shifts in macronutrient ratios and protein sources.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:37.240
Next up, a sparsely studied protein now tied to cellular senescence.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:44.400
Researchers linked the sparsely studied protein PTCHD4 to cellular senescence pathways.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:49.980
The work establishes a previously unreported connection in the literature on aging cells.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.060
Very little prior research had examined this protein in the context of senescence,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.660
leaving an open mechanistic node for further investigation.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:05.900
The PTCHD-4 finding identifies a protein with almost no prior aging literature as a direct

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:07.440
participant in senescence.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.820
Its near absence from earlier studies means researchers can now test whether modulating

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:17.560
this molecule alters the rate at which cells enter or exit the senescent state.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.560
Because the protein sits at a previously unmapped node, it could open routes to interventions

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.380
that complement existing senolytic approaches without overlapping their known targets.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
One question worth tracking is whether PTCHD4 levels in accessible tissues

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.000
track with other validated aging biomarkers in longitudinal human cohorts.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.400
On a different note, neurons express many genes longer than 100 kilobase pairs

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.980
and up to more than two megabase pairs that are essential for synapse formation.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:54.760
Standard transcription takes longer for these genes and requires additional regulatory steps.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.800
Researchers identified dedicated mechanisms that allow accurate and stable reading of these sequences.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:08.420
The work clarifies how neurons manage the extended time and regulatory demands of transcribing these exceptionally long genes.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.380
A new national study reveals that more than half of US adults have experienced physical violence

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.260
violence, and nearly half have experienced sexual violence in their lifetimes.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.600
The US National Study on Violence Experiences across the lifespan also shows stark disparities

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.300
in who experiences violence.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.140
Violence correlates closely with economic hardship, mental health issues, and substance use.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:38.160
The findings document lifetime prevalence across a representative adult sample and tie exposure

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:41.860
directly to measurable differences in economic and health outcomes.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:47.620
times. Adult chimpanzees in Senegal pass tools to young chimpanzees to help them learn

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:53.200
termite fishing techniques. The practice builds on observations first made by Jane Goodall

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:59.400
in the 1960s. Two-thirds of a century after the initial reports of tool use, the new data

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:05.080
show adults actively handing implements to juveniles during foraging. A new data set

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:10.420
from the US National Science Foundation National Center for Atmospheric Research supplies

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:15.140
city governments with neighborhood level heat information. The data combine urban

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:19.740
heat island effects with local weather patterns. Officials can use the maps to

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.160
target interventions that reduce heat-related health risks. The resource is

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.920
designed to help identify which city blocks face the greatest heat stress

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:32.400
during extreme events. Most people assume the head accounts for the largest

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:37.640
share of body heat loss. Right now, as you sit or move, your body sheds heat

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:42.880
primarily through the skin across the entire surface, with blood flow and sweating doing the

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.580
main work once core temperature rises. A single bout of moderate exercise can increase skin

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:54.740
blood flow by roughly tenfold, moving heat outward far more effectively than any localized exposure.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.340
The next time you feel overheated during daily activity, notice how quickly flushing and

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.880
perspiration spread across the torso and limbs rather than staying confined to the scalp.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:10.040
The practical takeaway is to focus cooling efforts on the whole body during heat waves,

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:16.840
with light clothing, shade, and hydration, rather than assuming a hat or head covering solves the problem.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:25.400
Future public health alerts that emphasize neighborhood level heat data will make these whole body strategies easier to time and target.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:32.760
An international team described Dinodontosaurus is Sea of Amundah from Tanzania, dating to roughly

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:39.520
240 million years ago. The fossil helps date one of the world's key Triassic sites. The

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.340
find may adjust estimates for the earliest dinosaur origins. Led by the University of Bristol,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:50.580
the discovery adds a new species to the record of early Arcosauriform reptiles.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:59.340
Yale researchers combined plasma with electro-catalysis to convert carbon dioxide into methanol, butane, and other products.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.820
The approach improves efficiency for practical gas to chemical conversion.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.500
Results appear in nature catalysis.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:14.980
The dual method system addresses long-standing challenges in turning captured carbon dioxide into usable fuels and feedstocks at scale.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:20.060
A McGill University and Universitate Laval study found that younger,

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:24.280
eco-anxious Canadians show higher tolerance for disruptive environmental

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:29.820
activism. Overall, Canadians reject vandalism and violence as protest methods.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.840
The findings are published in the Canadian Journal of Political Science. The survey

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.900
examined how emotional responses to climate change correlate with attitudes

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:44.260
toward different forms of activism. Excavations at UEDBET uncovered a

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:47.280
substantial built structure from the Second Millennium BC.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:52.700
The discovery indicates that Northwestern African communities actively modified their

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.800
environment and participated in Mediterranean networks.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:00.860
Research at the site is rewriting understanding of regional development during the Bronze

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:01.280
Age.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.720
USDA researchers developed predictive models to forecast where new world screwworm infestations

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:08.660
may occur next.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:13.880
The tools support earlier intervention and resource allocation for livestock, pets,

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:14.760
and wildlife.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:20.360
The Agricultural Research Service effort responds to recent detections in the United States

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:23.760
and aims to limit future economic and ecological damage.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:30.660
University of Utah researchers produced a comprehensive analysis of dust risks from the exposed great

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:31.820
Salt Lake Lake bed.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:38.120
The report outlines control options, associated costs, and long-term benefits of stabilizing

00:06:38.120 --> 00:06:39.880
lake levels for state agencies.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:45.880
Coordinated by the Wilkes Center for Climate Science and Policy, the document supplies data

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:48.900
for policy makers weighing dust mitigation strategies.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:55.080
A global study in nature water shows that identical rainfall on the same landscape can produce

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.240
different river flows at different times.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:01.960
The work maps how consistently catchments convert rain into stream flow.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.960
The findings demonstrate that catchment behavior is not fixed and that the same storm can yield

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:11.100
markedly different run-off depending on prior conditions.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:17.080
Scientists identified a bacterial protein that disrupts the developmental controls governing

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.080
flower formation.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:22.260
The mechanism turns reproductive organs into leaf-like structures.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.340
The pathogen is expected to expand northward with climate-driven insect factors.

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:33.560
Findings used synchrotron imaging at ESRF and appear in the Journal of Biological Chemistry.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:40.360
A TU Darmsch. team showed that completely disordered, constantly moving atoms can still emit light

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:46.400
preferentially in one direction. The degree of asymmetry can be tuned. The result is published

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.480
in physical review letters. The demonstration reveals that collective emission can be directed

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.660
even without ordered structure or external alignment. Keep an eye on follow-up work that tests whether

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:03.300
PTC HD4 levels track with established aging biomarkers in human cohorts.

